\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The composed pipeline as a DAG, which is a different graph from Fig.~\ref {fig:identification}}: that one is the \emph  {evaluation design}, this one is what a client update passes through. \textbf  {The statistic path} (green) is what a composition-invariance argument constrains: $d_1$'s per-client transform $T_1$ moves the points, $d_2$ computes $\sigma _2$ on the transformed points, $\sigma _2$ drives $d_2$'s decision, and the decision determines admission, i.e.\ levels (P1)--(P4) of Def.~\ref {def:levels_full} in order. \textbf  {The bypass arc} (red) is the one the paper's argument turns on: $d_1$'s weights reach $\Lambda _a$ (Cor.~\ref {cor:aggregate_mass}) without passing through $\sigma _2$ at all, so when $d_1$ zeroes an adversary's weight the adversary is excluded whatever $\sigma _2$ says and $d_1$ alone sets the outcome (Lem.~\ref {lem:annihilation}). Mode~S cuts that arc by pinning every adversary's coefficient at $c{=}1$, which is why it identifies the intervention's effect at a fixed adversarial coefficient share, \textbf  {not the statistic path in isolation}, since $T_1$ also moves the magnitude of what $d_2$ emits (Table~\ref {tab:channels}'s $\Delta $~agg.\ column), and also why it needs adversary identity and is an instrument rather than a defense. \textbf  {A statistic-level argument constrains the top path and leaves the bottom one free}, which is the whole of why (P1)--(P3) do not deliver (P5).}}{27}{figure.caption.31}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pipeline}{{A2}{27}{\textbf {The composed pipeline as a DAG, which is a different graph from Fig.~\ref {fig:identification}}: that one is the \emph {evaluation design}, this one is what a client update passes through. \textbf {The statistic path} (green) is what a composition-invariance argument constrains: $d_1$'s per-client transform $T_1$ moves the points, $d_2$ computes $\sigma _2$ on the transformed points, $\sigma _2$ drives $d_2$'s decision, and the decision determines admission, i.e.\ levels (P1)--(P4) of Def.~\ref {def:levels_full} in order. \textbf {The bypass arc} (red) is the one the paper's argument turns on: $d_1$'s weights reach $\Lambda _a$ (Cor.~\ref {cor:aggregate_mass}) without passing through $\sigma _2$ at all, so when $d_1$ zeroes an adversary's weight the adversary is excluded whatever $\sigma _2$ says and $d_1$ alone sets the outcome (Lem.~\ref {lem:annihilation}). Mode~S cuts that arc by pinning every adversary's coefficient at $c{=}1$, which is why it identifies the intervention's effect at a fixed adversarial coefficient share, \textbf {not the statistic path in isolation}, since $T_1$ also moves the magnitude of what $d_2$ emits (Table~\ref {tab:channels}'s $\Delta $~agg.\ column), and also why it needs adversary identity and is an instrument rather than a defense. \textbf {A statistic-level argument constrains the top path and leaves the bottom one free}, which is the whole of why (P1)--(P3) do not deliver (P5)}{figure.caption.31}{}}
