\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Why a transform's flip rate is not a sufficient statistic for what it does.} ASR against the \emph  {independently measured} disturbance of each arm's own statistic ($n{=}5$, Student-t 95\% intervals), the abscissa fixed before the freeze. Stars and crosses are the two \emph  {real} upstream transforms measured on these same $(d_2, \text  {attack})$ cells (NormClip, RFA), placed at their own measured disturbance rate: at matched disturbance they sit $+0.77$ and $+0.79$ ASR \emph  {above} the synthetic curve, while their realized weight ratios (mean $3.61$ and $11.93$, maximum $4.90$ and $17.38$) fall \emph  {inside} the ladder's span. The class-(a) arm's four rungs coincide at disturbance exactly $0$ ($0/120$ selections changed, maximum relative score drift $1.1\times 10^{-4}$) and its ASR falls anyway: Lemma~\ref {lem:annihilation}'s magnitude channel, not C2 disturbance.}}{31}{figure.caption.35}\protected@file@percent }
