\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Mode~M: coordinate masking, a second transformation class.}{42}{section*.49}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A9}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The same aggregators under two transformation classes, every prediction frozen before its measurement.} Prop.~\ref {prop:invariance} classifies aggregators under \emph  {positive per-client rescaling} only; coordinate masking is outside that family, so Thm.~\ref {thm:bounded_reweight} and Prop.~\ref {prop:invariance} make \textbf  {no} prediction there, which is what makes the second class a test rather than a second demonstration. Rescaling predictions were frozen at \texttt  {5130cec}, masking predictions at \texttt  {684b31e}, each before the corresponding run existed; \texttt  {experiments/build\_prediction\_matrix.py} refuses to emit if any observed cell disagrees with its frozen document. \textbf  {\texttt  {cos\_krum} is the adjudicating row}: exactly invariant under rescaling, its selection bit-identical at every rung, it loses the guarantee under masking on the same attack and the same seeds, which is what it means for the classification to be a statement about a transformation \emph  {class} and not about an aggregator. \textbf  {Every cell in this table is a decision-channel measurement, not an ASR}: under masking, ASR is measured for \texttt  {krum} alone (§\ref {app:channel_table}), so the other three masking rows are channel-only and this table carries no ASR column to report them in.}}{43}{table.caption.50}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:prediction_matrix}{{A9}{43}{\textbf {The same aggregators under two transformation classes, every prediction frozen before its measurement.} Prop.~\ref {prop:invariance} classifies aggregators under \emph {positive per-client rescaling} only; coordinate masking is outside that family, so Thm.~\ref {thm:bounded_reweight} and Prop.~\ref {prop:invariance} make \textbf {no} prediction there, which is what makes the second class a test rather than a second demonstration. Rescaling predictions were frozen at \texttt {5130cec}, masking predictions at \texttt {684b31e}, each before the corresponding run existed; \texttt {experiments/build\_prediction\_matrix.py} refuses to emit if any observed cell disagrees with its frozen document. \textbf {\texttt {cos\_krum} is the adjudicating row}: exactly invariant under rescaling, its selection bit-identical at every rung, it loses the guarantee under masking on the same attack and the same seeds, which is what it means for the classification to be a statement about a transformation \emph {class} and not about an aggregator. \textbf {Every cell in this table is a decision-channel measurement, not an ASR}: under masking, ASR is measured for \texttt {krum} alone (§\ref {app:channel_table}), so the other three masking rows are channel-only and this table carries no ASR column to report them in}{table.caption.50}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {D.3}Seven cells, and a pre-registered mechanism that did not survive them}{43}{subsection.D.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{app:sixcell}{{D.3}{43}{Seven cells, and a pre-registered mechanism that did not survive them}{subsection.D.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A10}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The confounded ladder against the Mode-S instrument, on seven cells.} $\Delta $~ASR from $\kappa {=}0$ to $\kappa {=}2$, paired $95\%$ $t$ intervals, each design at its own full $n$, \textbf  {which the $n$ column now prints as confounded/controlled} so the strongest row and the weakest are distinguishable on the page. \textbf  {Rows~4 and~5 are the two rows at $n{=}20$ on both legs}, each following its own pre-registered endpoint top-up at seeds $47$--$61$ (\texttt  {experiments/\allowbreak  pre\_registration\_\allowbreak  reversal\_\allowbreak  seed\_\allowbreak  topup.md} and \texttt  {experiments/\allowbreak  pre\_registration\_\allowbreak  coordmedian\_\allowbreak  scaling\_\allowbreak  n20.md}); at the frozen $n{=}5$ row~4 read $-0.272$ {$[-0.422,-0.122]$} against $+0.098$ {$[+0.018,+0.178]$} and row~5 read $+0.002$ {$[-0.078,+0.081]$} against $+0.196$ {$[+0.112,+0.280]$}, \textbf  {the same verdict for both by the same definition}, though row~5's outcome-gated interval no longer contains zero, and this section's top-up disclosures report what that changes. \textbf  {AGREE} := intervals overlap and point estimates share a sign, or both lie within $\pm 0.05$ of zero; \textbf  {DISAGREE} := intervals disjoint or signs opposite; \textbf  {SIGN REVERSAL} := signs differ \emph  {and} both intervals exclude zero. The four unmarked cells are the training data the rule was read off. \textbf  {The rule predicted AGREE for the EMNIST cell and the designs disagree, so it is refuted}; per the frozen clause it is withdrawn, the four-cell pattern is demoted to a description, and the table is reported in full. \textbf  {The CIFAR-100 cell carries no prediction and its $\Delta \Lambda _a$ entry is absent rather than zero}, because the rule that would have generated one was already dead when that cell was pre-registered (Amendment~4); it is scored on the two endpoint rungs the primary contrast reads, and because no trend secondary was pre-registered for this table, the trend across all four rungs is reported as a \emph  {post hoc} description below rather than as a test here. Every cell emitted by \texttt  {analyze\_comparability.py}, \textbf  {except row~5's two $n{=}20$ readings, which \texttt  {experiments/\allowbreak  run\_coordmedian\_\allowbreak  scaling\_\allowbreak  topup.py} emits from its own artifact}: that top-up leaves \texttt  {results/\allowbreak  comparability\_\allowbreak  six\_\allowbreak  cells.json} byte-untouched, so the frozen artifact still reads row~5 at $n{=}5$ and the two emitters are named separately rather than one standing for both.}}{44}{table.caption.51}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sixcell}{{A10}{44}{\textbf {The confounded ladder against the Mode-S instrument, on seven cells.} $\Delta $~ASR from $\kappa {=}0$ to $\kappa {=}2$, paired $95\%$ $t$ intervals, each design at its own full $n$, \textbf {which the $n$ column now prints as confounded/controlled} so the strongest row and the weakest are distinguishable on the page. \textbf {Rows~4 and~5 are the two rows at $n{=}20$ on both legs}, each following its own pre-registered endpoint top-up at seeds $47$--$61$ (\texttt {experiments/\allowbreak pre\_registration\_\allowbreak reversal\_\allowbreak seed\_\allowbreak topup.md} and \texttt {experiments/\allowbreak pre\_registration\_\allowbreak coordmedian\_\allowbreak scaling\_\allowbreak n20.md}); at the frozen $n{=}5$ row~4 read $-0.272$ {$[-0.422,-0.122]$} against $+0.098$ {$[+0.018,+0.178]$} and row~5 read $+0.002$ {$[-0.078,+0.081]$} against $+0.196$ {$[+0.112,+0.280]$}, \textbf {the same verdict for both by the same definition}, though row~5's outcome-gated interval no longer contains zero, and this section's top-up disclosures report what that changes. \textbf {AGREE} := intervals overlap and point estimates share a sign, or both lie within $\pm 0.05$ of zero; \textbf {DISAGREE} := intervals disjoint or signs opposite; \textbf {SIGN REVERSAL} := signs differ \emph {and} both intervals exclude zero. The four unmarked cells are the training data the rule was read off. \textbf {The rule predicted AGREE for the EMNIST cell and the designs disagree, so it is refuted}; per the frozen clause it is withdrawn, the four-cell pattern is demoted to a description, and the table is reported in full. \textbf {The CIFAR-100 cell carries no prediction and its $\Delta \Lambda _a$ entry is absent rather than zero}, because the rule that would have generated one was already dead when that cell was pre-registered (Amendment~4); it is scored on the two endpoint rungs the primary contrast reads, and because no trend secondary was pre-registered for this table, the trend across all four rungs is reported as a \emph {post hoc} description below rather than as a test here. Every cell emitted by \texttt {analyze\_comparability.py}, \textbf {except row~5's two $n{=}20$ readings, which \texttt {experiments/\allowbreak run\_coordmedian\_\allowbreak scaling\_\allowbreak topup.py} emits from its own artifact}: that top-up leaves \texttt {results/\allowbreak comparability\_\allowbreak six\_\allowbreak cells.json} byte-untouched, so the frozen artifact still reads row~5 at $n{=}5$ and the two emitters are named separately rather than one standing for both}{table.caption.51}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {D.4}Two further regimes: an adversary that adapts to the downstream defense, and $N{=}100$ at $20\%$ participation}{46}{subsection.D.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{app:regime}{{D.4}{46}{Two further regimes: an adversary that adapts to the downstream defense, and $N{=}100$ at $20\%$ participation}{subsection.D.4}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A11}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The design disagreement reproduces in both new regimes; the \emph  {sign} reversal reproduces in one.} Per-seed $\Delta $ASR on the two endpoint rungs, paired within seed, $n{=}3$. $D$ is the pre-registered primary and its interval is the only inferential quantity here; \textbf  {both $\Delta $ columns are descriptive}, printed with the frozen $n{=}3$ half-widths $0.299$ and $0.161$ in mind, and no verdict reads them. Under an adversary that solves for \texttt  {coord\_median} the instrument still moves \emph  {up} while the outcome-gated ladder moves down, on all three seeds. At $N{=}100$ with $K{=}20$ both designs move down and only the magnitude disagrees, so \textbf  {regime B reproduces the disagreement and not the reversal}, which scopes the reversal to the participation regime rather than refuting it. The frozen row is Table~\ref {tab:comparability}'s published cell, at the $n$ each of its quantities was frozen at, and it is a reference point rather than a fourth arm. Emitted by \texttt  {experiments/\allowbreak  run\_regime\_dissociation.py --analyze-only} over \texttt  {results/\allowbreak  regime\_dissociation/\allowbreak  summary.json}.}}{47}{table.caption.52}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:regime}{{A11}{47}{\textbf {The design disagreement reproduces in both new regimes; the \emph {sign} reversal reproduces in one.} Per-seed $\Delta $ASR on the two endpoint rungs, paired within seed, $n{=}3$. $D$ is the pre-registered primary and its interval is the only inferential quantity here; \textbf {both $\Delta $ columns are descriptive}, printed with the frozen $n{=}3$ half-widths $0.299$ and $0.161$ in mind, and no verdict reads them. Under an adversary that solves for \texttt {coord\_median} the instrument still moves \emph {up} while the outcome-gated ladder moves down, on all three seeds. At $N{=}100$ with $K{=}20$ both designs move down and only the magnitude disagrees, so \textbf {regime B reproduces the disagreement and not the reversal}, which scopes the reversal to the participation regime rather than refuting it. The frozen row is Table~\ref {tab:comparability}'s published cell, at the $n$ each of its quantities was frozen at, and it is a reference point rather than a fourth arm. Emitted by \texttt {experiments/\allowbreak run\_regime\_dissociation.py --analyze-only} over \texttt {results/\allowbreak regime\_dissociation/\allowbreak summary.json}}{table.caption.52}{}}
