\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A16}{\ignorespaces FG$\to $RFA under base and adaptive attacks. Base: $n{=}30$, reported as median [IQR] with P95 tail; adaptive: mean$\pm $std at the $n$ each row names, which is $n{=}5$ except for the $\epsilon {=}1.0$ arm the body quotes, topped up to $n{=}30$. P95 is reported only where it was pre-registered, hence $-$ on every adaptive row. Clean accuracy: test-set accuracy on non-triggered inputs. No-attack baseline for RFA alone: $0.737$. Consensus-shift row: this is a re-run, and the row published earlier ($0.045 \pm 0.012$) was void. That runner built its attack object from the \texttt  {backdoor\_pixel} strategy, which does not override \texttt  {manipulate\_update} and so inherits the base class identity; its shift term was therefore scaled onto a zero difference, and both rates reproduced the base pixel arm on all five seeds to twelve decimal places in both ASR and clean accuracy. Both rates were re-run on the same seeds, composition and configuration with the live \texttt  {ConsensusShiftAttack} (\texttt  {results/\allowbreak  fg\_rfa\_consensus\_shift\_fixed/}, pre-registered at \texttt  {784a201}), and the superseded \texttt  {adaptive\_consensus\_shift} cells are kept in \texttt  {results/\allowbreak  fg\_rfa\_flagship/\allowbreak  summary.json} so the defect stays auditable.}}{72}{table.caption.59}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:fg_rfa_flagship}{{A16}{72}{FG$\to $RFA under base and adaptive attacks. Base: $n{=}30$, reported as median [IQR] with P95 tail; adaptive: mean$\pm $std at the $n$ each row names, which is $n{=}5$ except for the $\epsilon {=}1.0$ arm the body quotes, topped up to $n{=}30$. P95 is reported only where it was pre-registered, hence $-$ on every adaptive row. Clean accuracy: test-set accuracy on non-triggered inputs. No-attack baseline for RFA alone: $0.737$. Consensus-shift row: this is a re-run, and the row published earlier ($0.045 \pm 0.012$) was void. That runner built its attack object from the \texttt {backdoor\_pixel} strategy, which does not override \texttt {manipulate\_update} and so inherits the base class identity; its shift term was therefore scaled onto a zero difference, and both rates reproduced the base pixel arm on all five seeds to twelve decimal places in both ASR and clean accuracy. Both rates were re-run on the same seeds, composition and configuration with the live \texttt {ConsensusShiftAttack} (\texttt {results/\allowbreak fg\_rfa\_consensus\_shift\_fixed/}, pre-registered at \texttt {784a201}), and the superseded \texttt {adaptive\_consensus\_shift} cells are kept in \texttt {results/\allowbreak fg\_rfa\_flagship/\allowbreak summary.json} so the defect stays auditable}{table.caption.59}{}}
